Radiculopathy – Lumbar
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Unilateral leg pain distal to the knee, Dermatomal sensory/motor deficit, Positive straight leg raise test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-051
Reason for Visit: Severe, acute-onset radiating leg pain causing extreme distress and an absolute inability to walk or sit.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing agonizing, electric-shock pain shooting from the lower back into the foot, accompanied by sudden leg weakness. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required to exclude acute cauda equina syndrome or progressive motor deficit (foot drop), and to administer immediate, potent neuropathic analgesia and muscle relaxants to restore basic, safe mobility before daytime hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "A bolt of lightning is shooting from my back down into my big toe; the pain is blinding, and my leg keeps giving way when I try to stand."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute, severe episode of left-sided lumbar radiculopathy (sciatica). Timeline: Experienced a minor "twinge" in the lower back yesterday while lifting a heavy box. Four hours ago, the patient developed a severe, unilateral, sharp, lancinating pain radiating from the lower back down the posterior aspect of the left leg. Crucially, the pain distinctly travels distal to the knee, extending deep into the calf and terminating in the big toe. Patient reports dense paresthesia rigidly following the L5 dermatome. Severe exacerbation of radiating pain is provoked by any attempt at sitting, coughing, or forceful Valsalva maneuvers. Reports sudden weakness when trying to lift the left foot while walking.
Functional Status: Acutely immobilized in a lateral decubitus position; unable to sit or stand due to severe nerve tension pain and leg yielding.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk secondary to pain inhibition and acute motor weakness.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/92 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Alert, diaphoretic, in severe distress, lying rigidly on the right side with the left knee flexed.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination of the lower extremities reveals distinct left-sided L5 nerve root compression. Sensory exam demonstrates decreased light touch and pinprick strictly over the dorsum of the left foot and big toe. Motor testing reveals 4/5 weakness specifically in left ankle dorsiflexion and great toe extension (L5 myotome - early foot drop). Reflex testing demonstrates normal and symmetrical patellar (L4) and Achilles (S1) deep tendon reflexes. Absolutely no saddle anesthesia or loss of anal sphincter tone (cauda equina ruled out). Strongly positive straight leg raise test on the left, eliciting severe radicular pain into the foot at 35 degrees of passive elevation. Patient exhibits a noticeable functional shift (antalgic lean) of the lumbar spine away from the left side when attempting to stand.
Environmental Observations: Heavy moving boxes present in the hallway.
Medication Reconciliation: Took regular Paracetamol and Ibuprofen with zero effect on the shooting nerve pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Left Lumbar Radiculopathy (L5 Nerve Root Compression/Sciatica) - Status: Acute/Severe
Differentials:

Cauda Equina Syndrome - Ruled out definitively by the strictly unilateral presentation, normal S1 reflexes, and absolute absence of saddle anesthesia or bowel/bladder dysfunction.

Trochanteric Bursitis - Ruled out by the electric, shooting nature of the pain traveling distally below the knee to the foot, the presence of hard neurological deficits (weakness/numbness), and the positive nerve tension signs.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out by the sudden mechanical onset, lack of diffuse calf swelling or erythema, and the clear dermatomal mapping of the pain and sensory loss.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a textbook, severe acute lumbar radiculopathy (L5), characterized by excruciating electric-shock pain extending below the knee, distinct dermatomal numbness, and specific myotomal weakness (foot drop). The critical clinical task was to rigorously examine the patient to exclude cauda equina syndrome. Once ruled out, the functional severity of the nerve compression—rendering the patient unable to walk safely and causing extreme distress—mandated immediate intervention. Delaying evaluation would have subjected the patient to unmanaged neuropathic agony and escalating panic. Urgent after-hours assessment safely confirmed the focal mechanical nature of the compression, provided diagnostic reassurance, and allowed for the rapid initiation of high-dose systemic steroids and neuropathic agents to decompress the inflamed nerve root.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Dexamethasone 8mg STAT (as a rapid systemic anti-inflammatory to reduce epidural nerve root edema). Prescribed oral Pregabalin 75mg BD to modulate the severe neuropathic shooting pain. Advised to continue Ibuprofen 400mg TDS.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Lie on the unaffected (right) side with a thick pillow placed firmly between the knees. Consequence: This position opens the neural foramina on the left side of the spine, physically creating more space for the trapped nerve and immediately reducing the agonizing electrical shocks (negative reinforcement).
Action: Strictly avoid sitting in soft chairs or couches for the next 48 hours; stand or lie down only. Consequence: Sitting places maximal pressure on the lower spinal discs, which will push the disc material further onto the nerve and trigger severe pain spikes (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): Provided a single crutch to assist with balance given the early foot drop.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48-72 hours to arrange a Lumbar Spine MRI to definitively identify the structural cause (e.g., extruded disc) and to monitor the L5 motor weakness.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is a sudden, total loss of movement in the leg, if the foot drops completely (flaccid paralysis), if numbness spreads to the groin/saddle area, or if there is any new loss of bowel or bladder control.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis securely identifies nerve root compression based on clinical neurology. An MRI is required to visualize the exact structural cause. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The electric pain should reduce significantly over 3-7 days as the steroids shrink the swelling around the trapped nerve. However, the numbness and weakness may take weeks to fully resolve depending on the severity of the compression.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care immediately if you suddenly lose the ability to lift your foot at all, if your groin becomes numb, or if you lose control of your bladder or bowels.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A bulging disc in your lower back is aggressively pinching the main nerve cable that supplies your leg (the L5 sciatic nerve). This is causing the electrical misfiring, numbness, and weakness in your foot. Standard painkillers do not work well for nerve compression. The high-dose steroid injection will rapidly shrink the swelling around the nerve, while the new nerve medication calms down the electrical shocks.
Actionable Steps: Do not sit down; lying on your side or standing are the only safe positions right now. Use the pillow between your knees to open the spine. You must see your regular doctor this week to organize an MRI scan of your back.

